    <comment>New package: bugwarden 0.2.0

Supersedes SR#1368076 (0.1.0), which was still in staging review; this is the
same new package at the newer upstream release.

An MCP (Model Context Protocol) server that exposes Bugzilla to LLM clients
behind a guard policy the client can neither read nor change. The policy is a
TOML file read once at startup and decides, per bug, which of thirteen
capabilities a client gets, so an operator can point an LLM client at a
Bugzilla instance while keeping undisclosed security bugs, private comments
and write access out of reach.

New in 0.2.0: the create_bug and add_attachment tools (capability vocabulary
grew from eleven to thirteen), and an optional append-only JSONL audit stream
that records every tool call together with the guard's verdict -- written only
when named with --audit-config / BUGWARDEN_AUDIT_CONFIG.

Rust, vendored with obs-service-cargo; the upstream test suite runs in %check
(249 tests, up from 129). Both worked example configurations are installed as
%config(noreplace) under /etc/bugwarden: policy.toml and audit.toml. Neither
is read implicitly -- the server only loads files named explicitly on the
command line or via the environment, so an unconfigured server cannot pick one
up.

License: is the aggregate over the crates statically linked into the binary
(unchanged from 0.1.0; recomputed against the new vendor set).</comment>
